\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Scope and generalizability.}
Our landscape characterization uses Pythia-410M as the primary architecture; the phase diagram is Pythia-family-only, frontier-scale behavior (${>}$7B) is untested, and cross-architecture evaluation is limited to $\alpha{=}1.0$.
All experiments use full replacement with a single decoding strategy (temperature~$1.0$, top-$k{=}50$); data accumulation~\citep{dey2024breaking} or lower temperatures would shift the landscape.
More fundamentally, the blind zone below $\alpha_c$ means that substantial quality degradation (e.g., ${\sim}40\%$ distinct-1 decline at $\alpha{=}0.25$) occurs before any statistical method signals detection.
The framework is therefore better characterized as a confirmation tool for ongoing collapse than an early-warning system.

\paragraph{Statistical power.}
PermG's empirical FPR is 6.7\% (CI $[1.8\%, 16.2\%]$) and $K{\geq}3$ consensus yields 10.0\% (6/60); per-condition detection rates carry wide binomial CIs (e.g., $[0.25, 0.92]$ for 6/8), so the phase diagram represents a coarse topology rather than precise probability estimates.

\paragraph{Causal interpretation.}
Permutation Granger testing establishes temporal precedence, not causation; our short-sequence regime ($T{\leq}25$) limits statistical power relative to econometric standards ($T{\geq}100$), and nonlinear alternatives to the linear PermG test yield no significant results in our setting (see \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

When language models train iteratively on their own output, the training distribution progressively loses its tail modes, a phenomenon termed model collapse~\citep{shumailov2024curse,alemohammad2024mad}.
This process can be triggered by vanishingly small synthetic fractions~\citep{dohmatob2025strong}: lexical diversity narrows, calibration deteriorates, and each generation inherits the biases of its predecessors.
As machine-generated text grows prevalent on the web, such feedback loops pose a growing risk to production pipelines, and by the time standard quality metrics register degradation, the distribution may have already shifted irreversibly.

This threat motivates a fundamental question: \emph{can we reliably detect the onset of model collapse?}
The answer is less clear than it might appear.
Iterative training produces short, noisy time series (often fewer than 15 points) where detection power drops sharply~\citep{boettiger2012limits}, and model collapse is a first-order transition~\citep{truong2026phase}, structurally ruling out the critical-slowing-down early-warning signals that ecologists rely on~\citep{scheffer2009early} (\S\ref{sec:related_work}).
More fundamentally, no prior work has systematically compared detection methods on this problem; as we show, the choice of statistical test alone can shift conclusions from universal detection to complete null on identical data.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_phase_diagram.pdf}
\caption{Phase diagram (Pythia-410M, fp32, PermG). FDR-significant canary$\to$diversity pairs across $\alpha$ and $T$; dashed curve: fitted boundary (Eq.~\ref{eq:alpha-c}); $\diamond$: non-monotonic anomaly; $\star$: dissolution at $T{=}25$.}
\label{fig:phase_diagram}
\end{figure}

We systematically map this landscape on Pythia-410M~\citep{biderman2023pythia} across 13 contamination levels with multi-seed replication (Figure~\ref{fig:phase_diagram}).
Our most consequential finding concerns method sensitivity: five statistical methods applied to identical data yield null-control FPR spanning 7\% to 94\% ($\kappa{=}0.185$; Figure~\ref{fig:method_comparison}), demonstrating that single-method analyses cannot reliably distinguish real signals from artifacts.
Permutation Granger causality (PermG), the lowest-FPR method (6.7\%, CI $[1.8\%, 16.2\%]$, 10~seeds), reveals structured terrain rather than smooth degradation: detection is absent through $\alpha{=}0.45$, then jumps from 0/8 to 6/8 significant pairs within a 2-percentage-point interval (Figure~\ref{fig:phase_diagram}), before a non-monotonic dip and eventual reliable detection above an empirical boundary $\alpha_c(T)$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:alpha-c}).
Cross-architecture evaluation at $\alpha{=}1.0$ across five families (345M--6.9B) supports forward detection in 11/15 conditions, with four non-confirming cases isolating distinct failure modes (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Our canary claim is scoped to diversity metrics (distinct-$n$~\citep{li2016diversity}); Granger precedence establishes temporal predictive improvement, not causation (\S\ref{sec:method}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{We document method sensitivity as a primary finding} (\S\ref{sec:mmct}).
    Five methods yield null-control FPR from 7\% to 94\% ($\kappa{=}0.185$); PermG, the lowest-FPR method across all five (6.7\%, CI $[1.8\%, 16.2\%]$, 10~seeds), is selected as the primary detector.

    \item \textbf{We map the detectability landscape} of iterative model collapse (\S\ref{sec:regime}--\ref{sec:dose_response}).
    Detection is absent through $\alpha{=}0.45$, then jumps from 0/8 to 6/8 within 2 percentage points; a detection boundary parametrized as $\alpha_c(T) \propto T^{-\gamma}$ ($\gamma \in [0.64, 2.08]$; Eq.~\ref{eq:alpha-c}) organizes three confirmed regimes (blind, onset, detection) plus one unexplained non-monotonic anomaly.

    \item \textbf{We empirically validate a cascade ordering for canary metric selection} (\S\ref{sec:cascade}).
    Under mode amplification, entropy responds before diversity by ${\geq}1$ generation (Principles~\ref{thm:entropy_leads}--\ref{prop:div_lag}), confirmed in 14/15 experiments ($\alpha \geq 0.30$; GPT-2-medium s43 bidirectional).
\end{enumerate}

